<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=microprocessor">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Microprocessor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as CPU on a chip</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A microprocessor is a computer processor whose data processing and control logic is contained on a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/integrated-circuit">integrated circuit</a>, or a small number of them. The first commercially available microprocessor was the Intel 4004, designed by Federico Faggin, Marcian Hoff, Stanley Mazor and Masatoshi Shima for the Japanese calculator maker Busicom. Production units went to Busicom in March 1971, and the first known advertisement is dated 15 November 1971. It was followed in 1972 by the 8-bit Intel 8008. Credit for the invention is disputed, and there are several rival designs from 1969 to 1971.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/microprocessor.jpg" alt="Microprocessor" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter1912 at German Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C4004_two_lines.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1971</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>United States</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Federico Faggin · Marcian Hoff · Stanley Mazor · Masatoshi Shima</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Garrett AiResearch Central Air Data Computer, whose chipset was complete by 1970, has been cited as an earlier microprocessor but was classified until 1998; the Four-Phase Systems AL1 of 1969 was later called one; Gilbert Hyatt's patent application of December 1970 led to a long dispute.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/integrated-circuit">Integrated circuit<span>1958</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/personal-computer">Personal computer<span>1972</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/smartphone">Smartphone<span>1992</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=microprocessor">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
